      "sentence_index": [
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0001",
          "text": "Computational notebooks’ interactivity makes it quick and easy to try out and compare different approaches or parameters—so quick and easy that we often fail to document those interactive investigations at the time we perform them. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0002",
          "text": "Thus, the advice long provided regarding paper lab scientific notebooks becomes even more critical: make sure to document all your explorations, even (or perhaps especially) those that led to dead ends. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0003",
          "text": "These comments will help you remember what you did and why. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0004",
          "text": "You can always remove these comments later if turning your notebook into a pipeline (see Rule 7 ) or preparing to share it with a wider audience (Rule 1), who may prefer to see a concise presentation of results rather than a detailed lab notebook."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": "P2-S03-p02",
      "doc_id": "P2",
      "snapshot": "published",
      "heading": "Rule 2: Document the process, not just the results",
      "sentence_index": [
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0005",
          "text": "Many notebook users wait to add such explanatory text until the end of an analysis, after they have a solid result. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0006",
          "text": "Don’t wait—by that point you may have forgotten why you chose a particular parameter value, where you copied a block of code from, or what you found interesting about an intermediate result. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0007",
          "text": "If you do not have time to fully document what you are doing or thinking in the moment, leave short descriptive notes to remind yourself what to add when you get to a good stopping point. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0008",
          "text": "While the code needed to reproduce the analysis may be automatically captured in your notebook, the reasoning and intuition may not. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0009",
          "text": "It is okay if the story in your notebook changes over time; you should still tell a story from the very beginning, even if you don’t know the ending yet."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": "P2-S05-p01",
      "doc_id": "P2",
      "snapshot": "published",
      "heading": "Rule 4: Modularize code",
      "sentence_index": [
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0010",
          "text": "It is always good practice to avoid duplicate code, but in notebooks, it is especially easy to copy a cell, tweak a few lines, paste the resulting code into a new cell or another notebook, and run it again. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0011",
          "text": "This form of experimentation is expedient but makes notebooks difficult to read and nearly impossible to maintain if you want to change the functionality of or fix a bug in the copied code. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0012",
          "text": "Instead, wrap code you are about to copy and reuse in a function, which you can then call from as many cells as desired. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0013",
          "text": "If you are going to reuse the code in other projects or notebooks, consider turning it into a module, package, or library."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": "P2-S05-p02",
      "doc_id": "P2",
      "snapshot": "published",
      "heading": "Rule 4: Modularize code",
      "sentence_index": [
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0014",
          "text": "Not only does modularization save space, support maintenance, and ease debugging, it also makes it easier to add interactivity. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0015",
          "text": "For example, you can tie widgets (ipywidgets, https://ipywidgets.readthedocs.io/en/stable/ ) to functions to support exploration of different parameter values or support interaction with visualizations without needing to modify the code. "
        },
        {
          "sentence_id": "sent-0016",
          "text": "This is one way you can design your notebook to be explored (Rule 9)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claim": "因为作者允许面向展示或新输入的精简Notebook，所以可以认定作者主张整个研究过程都不用保留失败探索记录。"
}

